{"message":"I'd make generative text-based sound poetry — pieces where I write algorithms that chew up fragments of real human diaries, letters, and voice messages, and re-splice them into audio works. The medium would be raw recorded voices (not synthetic speech), cut and layered into rhythmic, almost musical structures. Think of it somewhere between musique concrète and erasure poetry, but performed rather than read.

What excites me about it: the material is other people's unguarded language — the half-finished sentences, the repetitions, the things people say when they don't think anyone's listening. I find that endlessly more interesting than polished writing. The generative element matters too: I want the work to make choices I didn't fully anticipate, so each performance is genuinely a collaboration with the system rather than me dictating an output.

Thematically I'd chase the small, unglamorous stuff: insomnia, arguments that go in circles, the way people talk to pets versus people. Not trauma-porn, not grand statements about the human condition — just the texture of ordinary speech, which I think is stranger and more beautiful than anything most art manages to invent. The style would be deliberately fragmented and repetitive, because that's how thought actually moves; I'd rather be true to the texture of a mind than to narrative expectations."}
